# NOTICE

## Vendored code that must not be published

`packages/ccompactor/src/compact/vendor/` contains code copied from
[openclaude](https://github.com/Gitlawb/openclaude), a community fork of
Anthropic's Claude Code CLI, by `scripts/extract-openclaude-compact.ts`.

openclaude's own LICENSE states:

> The original Claude Code source is proprietary software:
> Copyright (c) Anthropic PBC. All rights reserved. Subject to Anthropic's
> Commercial Terms of Service. … The underlying derived code remains subject to
> Anthropic's copyright. This project does not have Anthropic's authorization
> to distribute their proprietary source.

**Therefore that directory must never reach a registry.** It exists to reach
behavioural parity quickly while the clean-room implementation in
`packages/ccompactor/src/compact/engine.ts` is written. That file implements the
same nine-section contract from its published description, with ccompactor's own
wording, and imports nothing from `vendor/`.

Two things make the replacement a plan rather than a hope:

1. `packages/ccompactor/scripts/guard-vendor.mjs` runs on `prepublishOnly` and
   **fails the publish** while vendored files are present.
2. Everything outside `engine.ts` talks to `summarise()` and never to the
   vendored module, so switching implementations is a change to one line.

To delete the vendored code once parity is confirmed:

```sh
rm -rf packages/ccompactor/src/compact/vendor
# then switch the delegate in src/compact/engine.ts to summariseWithContract
```

## What ccompactor owes to sctxx

The product framing — cross-agent handoff, provenance on every claim, a verify
step, layered artifacts — follows [sctxx](https://github.com/handyutils/sctxx)
by the same author. None of its code is used; sctxx is Rust and ccompactor is
TypeScript, and the two agree on behaviour rather than implementation.

## What ccompactor owes to published research

The compaction-side design follows the same survey sctxx's `docs/research/`
records: observation masking as the cheapest strategy with an ablation
(arXiv:2508.21433), addressable recall as what makes dropping safe
(arXiv:2607.25066), and the finding that no published result measures a handoff
to a different agent (arXiv:2606.02875).
